{"root":"خ س ر","decision":"used","hebrew":["חסר","חשר"],"reason":"Hebrew/Aramaic חסר: sound correspondence only, supported for the Syriac branch by Torrey as reported in Corpus Coranicum (Peshitta ḥsar for ἀπόλλυμι, Luke 9:24). Its range is lack/want/decrease (Ps 23:1; Deut 2:7; Prov 28:27; Aramaic Dan 5:27 חַסִּיר 'found wanting' on the scales; Eccl 4:8 מְחַסֵּר beside עָמֵל). Used for the solitary toiler (paragraph 2) and short weight (paragraph 23). חשר is a false friend: BDB cites an Arabic cognate meaning 'collect' (nave, gathering of clouds), unrelated to loss.","paragraphs":[2,23],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001","S103-TEV-SYD-009"]}
{"root":"ص ب ر","decision":"used","hebrew":["צבר"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only. Hebrew צבר means only 'heap up' (8 WLC occurrences: Gen 41:35, 41:49; Exod 8:10; Job 27:16; Ps 39:7; Hab 1:10; Zech 9:3; 2 Kgs 10:8 צִבֻּרִים); the senses of holding back, enduring and patience are entirely absent, so for ṣabr proper it is a false friend. It matches only the subra 'heap bought without measure' of paragraph 35, where Gen 41:49 (heaped grain until counting stopped) was used. Hebrew expresses patience with אֶרֶךְ אַפַּיִם (Prov 16:32), used separately.","paragraphs":[35],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-012"]}
{"root":"ص ل ح","decision":"used","hebrew":["צלח"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only. Hebrew צלח mostly means 'advance, prosper, succeed' (Josh 1:8; Ps 1:3; Isa 55:11; Aramaic Ezra 6:14), plus 'rush upon' of the spirit (Judg 14:6) and 'be fit, useful' (Jer 13:7, 13:10; Ezek 15:4). The 'fit for use' sense in Jer 13:7 stands against נִשְׁחַת 'spoiled', matching the salāḥ/fasād contrast of paragraph 13; Ps 1:3 gives the prospering of lasting works for paragraph 14. The peace-making (ṣulḥ) sense is absent in Hebrew.","paragraphs":[13,14],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-007","S103-TEV-MTF-007"]}
{"root":"ع ص ر","decision":"used","hebrew":["עצר"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only. Hebrew עצר means 'restrain, retain, shut up' (Gen 16:2; 1 Kgs 8:35; Ps 106:30; Job 4:2 restraining words; Jer 20:9 fire shut up in the bones), with מַעְצָר 'self-control' (Prov 25:28) and עֲצָרָה 'solemn assembly'. It supports the 'holding back' sense of ʿaṣr noted in paragraph 29 and the boundary image of paragraph 30; the Arabic senses 'press, squeeze', 'late afternoon' and 'era' are absent.","paragraphs":[29,30,37],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-010","S103-TEV-SYD-011","S103-TEV-MTF-021"]}
{"root":"ع م ل","decision":"used","hebrew":["עמל"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only. Hebrew עמל is skewed toward wearying toil, trouble and misery (Job 5:7; Eccl 1:3, 2:11, 2:22; Ps 127:1), with עָמֵל 'labourer, sufferer'; the neutral 'intentional deed' and the commercial 'wage/dealing' senses of paragraphs 10-11 are absent (Hebrew uses מַעֲשֶׂה, פֹּעַל, שָׂכָר). Used to mark the semantic shift (paragraph 10) and the solitary toiler of Eccl 4:8-9 (paragraph 2).","paragraphs":[2,10],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001","S103-TEV-SYD-006"]}
{"root":"و ص ي","decision":"no_hebrew_cognate","hebrew":[],"reason":"No Hebrew or Aramaic root with corresponding consonants exists in the lexicon. The Hebrew verb of the parallel deathbed and fatherly charges is צוה (Gen 18:19 יְצַוֶּה; Gen 49:29, 49:33), which matches وصّى in meaning; any genetic link would require a metathesis of the first two radicals and is proposed only from knowledge, unverified by the lexicon (BDB in this edition gives no cognate note). The Bible's usage of צוה is cited in the parallels at paragraph 20 as a semantic equivalent, not as a cognate; the Arabic plant-joining senses of paragraphs 16-17 have no Hebrew counterpart.","paragraphs":[20],"annotations":[]}

